<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narathu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1171</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narathu was king of Pagan from 1167 to about 1171, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungsithu">Alaungsithu</a>, whom tradition says he killed. Remembered in Burmese chronicles for his cruelty, he began the Dhammayangyi, the most massive temple at Pagan, which was left unfinished. He was assassinated after a few years on the throne, and his successor was soon killed as well.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Narathu was a middle son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungsithu">Sithu I</a> and Queen Yadanabon, whose father, Dhamma Kyin, had been a minister at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyansittha">Kyansittha</a>; the four main Burmese chronicles disagree on the dates of his life. His elder brother Min Shin Saw was heir apparent, but after a falling out with their father he was sent into exile at Aung Pinle Lake, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>. Narathu then impressed the king with his handling of the kingdom's daily affairs and became heir apparent in all but name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the king fell violently ill in 1167, Narathu had him moved to the Shwegugyi Temple, which Sithu had built in 1131. The chronicles say that the king woke, was furious to find himself out of the palace, and that Narathu then put a blanket over his head. Min Shin Saw came with an army to claim the throne; Narathu submitted and led his coronation, and the new king was poisoned at his first meal. Deeply unpopular, Narathu shut himself in his palace and built the Dhammayangyi, the largest temple of Pagan, to atone for his sins. In a fit of rage he killed with his bare hands a queen who was a daughter of the chief of Pateikkaya, a tributary kingdom to the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1171 the chief of Pateikkaya sent eight assassins disguised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> astrologers, who hid swords under their robes, gained an audience and killed the king, then took their own lives when the guards rushed in. Narathu is therefore remembered as Kalaja Min, the king who fell by the kalars. The historian Gordon Luce proposed that assassins from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a> killed him instead, a theory Htin Aung rejected as conjecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narathu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1161" aria-label="Bagan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Narathu"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 5744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 5744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1161">What was here in 1161? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
